# Intelligent Condition Monitoring of HV Isolator Using MATLAB/Simulink

## Project Overview

This project develops a MATLAB/Simulink model for condition monitoring of a motor-operated high-voltage isolator.

The system models the electrical drive and mechanical operating mechanism of the isolator. Electrical and mechanical signals are collected during each opening/closing operation and are later used for feature extraction, fault analysis, dataset generation, and ANN/ML-based health prediction.

The present implementation contains:

- Healthy isolator simulation
- Mechanical friction-fault simulation
- Motor voltage measurement
- Motor current measurement
- Shaft/mechanism torque measurement
- Angular-position measurement
- Shaft-speed measurement for fault analysis
- Fault-torque generation
- Feature extraction
- Healthy dataset generation
- Healthy/faulty waveform comparison
- Preparation of data for machine-learning/ANN training

The project has been developed for **MATLAB R2026a**.

---

# 1. Software Requirements

Recommended MATLAB installation:

- MATLAB R2026a
- Simulink
- Simscape
- Simscape Electrical

For later ANN/ML development:

- Deep Learning Toolbox
- Statistics and Machine Learning Toolbox

---

# 2. Main Models

## `coremodel.slx`

Main healthy isolator model.

This model represents the normal operating condition of the isolator mechanism.

Typical logged signals are:

| Signal | Description | Unit |
|---|---|---|
| `Vm` | Motor terminal voltage | V |
| `Im` | Motor current | A |
| `tauNm` | Mechanical/shaft torque | N·m |
| `theta_deg` | Isolator angular position | degree |

This model should be used to generate the baseline/healthy dataset.

---

## `coremodelfault.slx`

Fault-injected version of the isolator model.

The model contains an additional mechanical fault branch used to represent increased shaft friction/bearing resistance.

In addition to the normal signals, the model may log:

| Signal | Description | Unit |
|---|---|---|
| `omega` | Shaft angular velocity | rad/s |
| `Tfault` | Additional generated fault torque | N·m |

The faulty simulation is compared with the healthy model to determine the effect of increased mechanical resistance on voltage, current, torque, operating time, shaft speed, and position.

---

# 3. Important Simulation Parameters

A typical simulation uses:

```matlab
Bshaft = 2000;
torquefullscaleNm = 1000;
```

`Bshaft` represents the shaft/mechanical damping or friction-related parameter used by the model.

`torquefullscaleNm` represents the full-scale torque value used in the measurement/ADC processing chain.

Other configuration values are initialized by `init.m` and/or the project configuration files.

Always run the initialization script before starting the simulation.

---

# 4. Running the Project

Open MATLAB R2026a and make the project directory the MATLAB **Current Folder**.

Run:

```matlab
clear;
clc;
close all;

run("init.m");
```

Set the required parameters if they are not already defined by `init.m`:

```matlab
Bshaft = 2000;
torquefullscaleNm = 1000;
```

---

# 5. Running the Healthy Model

Run:

```matlab
outHealthy = sim("coremodel","StopTime","12");
```

Obtain the logged signals:

```matlab
logsHealthy = outHealthy.logsout;
```

Check which signals were logged:

```matlab
disp(string(logsHealthy.getElementNames))
```

Expected signals include:

```text
Vm
Im
tauNm
theta_deg
```

Example:

```matlab
Vm_sig    = logsHealthy.get("Vm");
Im_sig    = logsHealthy.get("Im");
tau_sig   = logsHealthy.get("tauNm");
theta_sig = logsHealthy.get("theta_deg");
```

Each signal must use its own time vector.

For example:

```matlab
tV = Vm_sig.Values.Time;
Vm = squeeze(Vm_sig.Values.Data);

tI = Im_sig.Values.Time;
Im = squeeze(Im_sig.Values.Data);

tTau = tau_sig.Values.Time;
tauNm = squeeze(tau_sig.Values.Data);

tTheta = theta_sig.Values.Time;
theta_deg = squeeze(theta_sig.Values.Data);
```

Do **not** truncate all signals to the same number of samples because Simscape signals may have different time vectors.

---

# 6. Running the Fault Model

Run:

```matlab
outFault = sim("coremodelfault","StopTime","12");
```

Obtain the logged dataset:

```matlab
logsFault = outFault.logsout;
```

Check its contents:

```matlab
disp(string(logsFault.getElementNames))
```

Expected signals include:

```text
Vm
Im
tauNm
theta_deg
omega
Tfault
```

The exact set depends on which Simulink signals have signal logging enabled.

---

# 7. Healthy Dataset Generation

The healthy model is executed repeatedly while selected model parameters are varied within an acceptable healthy range.

Important measurements are collected from each operation.

Typical extracted quantities include:

- Mean motor voltage
- Minimum motor voltage
- Voltage span
- RMS motor current
- Peak motor current
- Mean absolute torque
- Maximum torque
- Torque standard deviation
- Operating time
- Final angular position
- Current energy-related features
- Torque-related features

The extracted features form the healthy-condition dataset used as a reference for later fault classification.

---

# 8. Feature Extraction

`operation_features.m` performs operation-level feature calculation.

Its input measurements correspond to quantities such as:

```text
Motor Voltage
Motor Current
Mechanism Torque
Mechanism Position
```

The function generates an operation feature vector that can be used by the later ANN/ML stage.

`featuresextraction.m` is used for processing simulation results and forming the required feature dataset.

`featurecheck.m` is used for examining the generated features and verifying that they remain physically meaningful.

---

# 9. Healthy and Faulty Comparison

The main objective of the fault experiment is to compare:

```text
Healthy isolator
       ↓
V, I, Torque, Position
       ↓
Baseline characteristics

Faulty isolator
       ↓
Additional mechanical resistance
       ↓
V, I, Torque, Position, Speed, Fault Torque
       ↓
Fault characteristics
```

Increasing mechanical resistance is expected to alter quantities such as motor current, torque, mechanism speed, and operating time.

The exact effect should be determined from the simulated waveforms rather than assumed beforehand.

---

# 10. Visualization

Run the supplied visualization script:

```matlab
run("visualize_healthy_faulty.m")
```

The script generates:

1. Healthy-condition voltage waveform
2. Healthy-condition current waveform
3. Healthy-condition torque waveform
4. Healthy-condition angular-position waveform
5. Fault-condition voltage waveform
6. Fault-condition current waveform
7. Fault-condition torque waveform
8. Fault-condition angular-position waveform
9. Healthy versus faulty voltage comparison
10. Healthy versus faulty current comparison
11. Healthy versus faulty torque comparison
12. Healthy versus faulty angle comparison
13. Shaft-speed waveform, when available
14. Generated fault-torque waveform, when available

